                "**Alivus Life Sciences is still the one to own.** It is written into more customer approvals than anyone here (617 master files and certificates at June 2026), its last US inspections were clean (minor voluntary fixes at Ankleshwar, none at Dahej), and its filed operating margin has held at 29–34% for five quarters while ₹880cr of cash and short-term investments built up. In Q1 FY27 sales to customers other than Glenmark rose 26.5%, about 20% in constant currency and almost all of it volume, which is what a filing moat looks like. For the first time management gave revenue guidance, 10–12% for FY27, but it leans on the second half: after Q1's ₹640cr it needs about ₹720–740cr a quarter, more than any quarter so far. The price needs about {need:Alivus Life Sciences|17}% a year of earnings growth against my ~15%, which keeps it just inside {Label:Alivus Life Sciences|Fits}; Glenmark's orders (29% of FY26 sales, down 52.6% in Q1, and on longer credit that has stretched the cash cycle from 190 to 267 days) and the Solapur plant, now due by Diwali, are what I will watch.",
